#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:2
msgid "Marker: Points and Lines (C++)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:4
msgid ""
"**Goal:** Show how to use ``visualization_msgs/msg/Marker`` messages to send "
"points and lines to RViz."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:6
msgid "**Tutorial level:** Intermediate"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:8
msgid "**Time:** 15 Minutes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:12
msgid "Contents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:16
msgid ""
"This tutorial assumes that you have completed :doc:`Marker: Sending Basic "
"Shapes <../Marker-Sending-Basic-Shapes/Marker-Sending-Basic-Shapes>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:19
msgid "Intro"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:20
msgid ""
"In :doc:`Marker: Sending Basic Shapes <../Marker-Sending-Basic-Shapes/Marker-"
"Sending-Basic-Shapes>` you learned how to send simple shapes to RViz using "
"visualization markers. You can send more than just simple shapes, and this "
"tutorial introduces the ``POINTS``, ``LINE_STRIP``, and ``LINE_LIST`` marker "
"types. For a full list of types, see :doc:`Marker: Display types <../Marker-"
"Display-types/Marker-Display-types>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:25
msgid "Using Points, Line Strips, and Line Lists"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:26
msgid ""
"The ``POINTS``, ``LINE_STRIP``, and ``LINE_LIST`` markers all use the "
"``points`` member of the ``visualization_msgs/msg/Marker`` message. The "
"``POINTS`` type places a point at each point added. The ``LINE_STRIP`` type "
"uses each point as a vertex in a connected set of lines, where point 0 is "
"connected to point 1, 1 to 2, 2 to 3, and so on. The ``LINE_LIST`` type "
"creates unconnected lines out of each pair of points, such as point 0 to 1, "
"2 to 3, and so on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:33
msgid "The code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:34
msgid ""
"Get the package from the `visualization_tutorials repository <https://github."
"com/ros-visualization/visualization_tutorials>`_. The code for this tutorial "
"lives in the ``visualization_marker_tutorials`` package. You can read it in "
"`points_and_lines.cpp <https://github.com/ros-visualization/"
"visualization_tutorials/blob/ros2/visualization_marker_tutorials/src/"
"points_and_lines.cpp>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:39
msgid "The code explained"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:40
msgid ""
"Now let's break down the code, skipping things that were explained in the "
"previous tutorial. The overall effect created is a rotating helix with lines "
"sticking upwards from each vertex."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:43
msgid ""
"We start with the headers used by the node, including ``cmath`` for the "
"helix and the messages used for markers and points."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:45
msgid ""
"#define _USE_MATH_DEFINES\n"
"\n"
"#include <chrono>\n"
"#include <cmath>\n"
"#include <memory>\n"
"\n"
"#include \"rclcpp/rclcpp.hpp\"\n"
"#include \"geometry_msgs/msg/point.hpp\"\n"
"#include \"visualization_msgs/msg/marker.hpp\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:57
msgid ""
"This should look familiar. We initialize ROS 2, create a node, create a "
"publisher on the ``visualization_marker`` topic, and set the loop rate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:60
msgid ""
"rclcpp::init(argc, argv);\n"
"auto node = rclcpp::Node::make_shared(\"points_and_lines\");\n"
"auto marker_pub = node->create_publisher<visualization_msgs::msg::Marker>(\n"
"  \"visualization_marker\", 10);\n"
"rclcpp::Rate loop_rate(30);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:68
msgid ""
"We also create a floating-point variable that will be used to animate the "
"helix over time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:70
msgid "float f = 0.0f;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:74
msgid ""
"Inside the main loop, we create three ``visualization_msgs/msg/Marker`` "
"messages and initialize all of their shared data. By default, a marker "
"message contains a pose whose quaternion is initialized to the identity "
"orientation, so we only need to set the fields that matter for this tutorial."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:77
msgid ""
"visualization_msgs::msg::Marker points, line_strip, line_list;\n"
"points.header.frame_id = line_strip.header.frame_id = line_list.header."
"frame_id = \"my_frame\";\n"
"points.header.stamp = line_strip.header.stamp = line_list.header.stamp = "
"rclcpp::Clock().now();\n"
"points.ns = line_strip.ns = line_list.ns = \"points_and_lines\";\n"
"points.action = line_strip.action = line_list.action = visualization_msgs::"
"msg::Marker::ADD;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:85
msgid ""
"Here we assign three different IDs to the three markers. The use of the "
"``points_and_lines`` namespace ensures they will not collide with other "
"marker publishers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:88
msgid ""
"points.id = 0;\n"
"line_strip.id = 1;\n"
"line_list.id = 2;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:94
msgid ""
"Here we set the marker types to ``POINTS``, ``LINE_STRIP``, and "
"``LINE_LIST``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:96
msgid ""
"points.type = visualization_msgs::msg::Marker::POINTS;\n"
"line_strip.type = visualization_msgs::msg::Marker::LINE_STRIP;\n"
"line_list.type = visualization_msgs::msg::Marker::LINE_LIST;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:102
msgid ""
"The ``scale`` member means different things for these marker types. "
"``POINTS`` markers use the ``x`` and ``y`` members for width and height "
"respectively, while ``LINE_STRIP`` and ``LINE_LIST`` markers use only the "
"``x`` component, which defines the line width. Scale values are in meters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:106
msgid ""
"points.scale.x = 0.2;\n"
"points.scale.y = 0.2;\n"
"\n"
"line_strip.scale.x = 0.1;\n"
"line_list.scale.x = 0.1;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:114
msgid ""
"Here we set the points to green, the line strip to blue, and the line list "
"to red. As with other markers, the alpha channel must be non-zero."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:117
msgid ""
"points.color.g = 1.0f;\n"
"points.color.a = 1.0;\n"
"\n"
"line_strip.color.b = 1.0;\n"
"line_strip.color.a = 1.0;\n"
"\n"
"line_list.color.r = 1.0;\n"
"line_list.color.a = 1.0;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:128
msgid ""
"Now we create the vertices for the points and lines. We use sine and cosine "
"to generate a helix. The ``POINTS`` and ``LINE_STRIP`` markers both require "
"only one point for each vertex, while the ``LINE_LIST`` marker requires two "
"points for each line segment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:132
msgid ""
"for (uint32_t i = 0; i < 100; ++i) {\n"
"  float y = 5 * sin(f + i / 100.0f * 2 * M_PI);\n"
"  float z = 5 * cos(f + i / 100.0f * 2 * M_PI);\n"
"\n"
"  geometry_msgs::msg::Point p;\n"
"  p.x = static_cast<int32_t>(i) - 50;\n"
"  p.y = y;\n"
"  p.z = z;\n"
"\n"
"  points.points.push_back(p);\n"
"  line_strip.points.push_back(p);\n"
"\n"
"  // The line list needs two points for each line\n"
"  line_list.points.push_back(p);\n"
"  p.z += 1.0;\n"
"  line_list.points.push_back(p);\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:152
msgid "Once the marker messages are filled out, we publish all three of them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:154
msgid ""
"marker_pub->publish(points);\n"
"marker_pub->publish(line_strip);\n"
"marker_pub->publish(line_list);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:160
msgid "Then we sleep, advance the animation phase, and loop back to the top."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Tutorials/Intermediate/RViz/Marker-Points-and-Lines/Marker-Points-and-Lines.rst:162
msgid ""
"loop_rate.sleep();\n"
"f += 0.04f;"
msgstr ""

